WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:11.720
[REQUIREMENT I OF V  //  IDENTIFICATION OF THE PROBLEM(S) ADDRESSED [1-4]] Requirement One: Identification of the Problems Addressed. The two graphs on the left show why sharing high-speed artificial intelligence servers across defense programs is broken today. Traditional cybersecurity stops at network cables and operating system software, leaving the physical memory wires inside the server completely unguarded. As the top graph shows, under the CXL 3.1 standard, four memory requests travel together in a single bundle. When legacy hardware detects less than one percent of unauthorized traffic, it poisons the entire bundle, destroying three innocent neighbor requests and crashing usable server speed to 48.9 percent. And as the bottom chart shows, checking permissions with standard memory lookup tables wastes 250 gigabytes of memory in every rack, costing 37.5 million dollars across ten thousand racks. What does this actually cost the mission? You cannot consolidate coalition AI programs onto shared forward servers if one compromised container can either steal neighbor data or cut cluster throughput in half. (CITATIONS: [1] CXL Consortium Spec v3.1, Ch. 3 (512b Flit Retry)  •  [2] M. A. Thornton, arXiv:2608.17139v1 (2026, Stateful Directory Tax)  •  [3] R. Landauer, Physics Today 44(5) (1991)  •  [4] E. C. Murphy, Zenodo DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.24047902 (2026))

00:01:11.720 --> 00:02:20.320
[REQUIREMENT II OF V  //  TECHNOLOGY SOLUTION & DOD APPLICATION [4-7]] Requirement Two: Description of the Technology Solution and its Application to DoD. The diagram at the top left shows what happens inside the Spectral Flit-Filter on every memory transfer. Instead of trusting the host operating system, our hardware core sits directly on the memory wire, managed by a standard Kubernetes plugin that locks in mission rules in 1.36 milliseconds. When four neighbor slots arrive together, our filter checks all four in sixteen nanoseconds using combinational logic with zero stored memory tables. The unauthorized write in Slot 1 is surgically turned into a harmless no-op, and the unauthorized AI weight read in Slot 3 is scrubbed to zeroes on the wire, while allied programs in Slots 0 and 2 keep running at 99.2 percent speed. Why can't an operating system hack or warm reboot turn this off? Because the hardware ratchet is tied to the physical cold power-on reset pin, trading unbounded page tables for 64 structured cohorts so the entire lock fits in zero Block RAM. (CITATIONS: [4] Murphy, DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.24047902 (Thm. 3.5 & Sec. 5)  •  [5] AMD UltraScale+ CLB User Guide UG574 (LUT6_2 O5/O6 SRAM Frame)  •  [6] PCI-SIG PCIe 6.0 / CXL 3.1 DVSEC Spec  •  [7] NIST SP 800-207 (Zero Trust) & NSA NCDSMO RAIN)

00:02:20.320 --> 00:03:31.850
[REQUIREMENT III OF V  //  POTENTIAL IMPACT IF ADOPTED BY DOD [4, 8, 9]] Requirement Three: Potential Impact if Adopted by the Department of Defense. Look at the delay curve at the top left. As a shared AI cluster approaches full load, traditional memory permission tables suffer cache misses that spike delay to 184.2 nanoseconds, stalling expensive GPU clusters. Because the Spectral Flit-Filter uses zero lookup tables, its delay line stays completely flat at 8 nanoseconds across all loads. In the bottom chart, you see the verified results from 900,000 live transfers on commercial 16-nanometer cloud hardware: 100 percent of rogue writes blocked, zero system crashes, 37.5 million dollars in saved memory hardware, and up to 39 megawatts of power saved per ten thousand racks. Where is the tactical multiplier at the edge? Because our circuit contains zero onboard memory cells, cosmic rays and high-altitude radiation have no tables to corrupt, eliminating over 99.98 percent of radiation bit-flip errors for satellites and tactical aircraft. (CITATIONS: [8] F. Poloczek & F. Ciucu, IEEE INFOCOM (2014, Martingale Tail Bounds)  •  [9] JEDEC JESD89A (SEU Rate) & RTCA DO-254 DAL-A  •  [4] Murphy, DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.24047902 (Thm. 6.2 & Thm. 6.3, Live 16nm Silicon Receipts))

00:03:31.850 --> 00:04:54.250
[REQUIREMENT IV OF V  //  DIFFERENTIATION FROM OTHER SOLUTIONS [10-12]] Requirement Four: Differentiation from Other Technology and Service Solutions. Both the grouped bar chart at the top and the comparison matrix at the bottom contrast the Spectral Flit-Filter directly against the three existing solutions available to the government today. First, compared to software container tools, eBPF service meshes, and hypervisors: software runs inside the host operating system, so if an attacker gains administrator privileges or reboots the host, software security drops to zero. Our lock sits in physical hardware outside the operating system. Second, compared to standard CPU memory guards and lookup tables: they waste 250 gigabytes of memory per rack and add 184 nanoseconds of delay, whereas our filter wastes zero gigabytes and responds over ten times faster. Third, compared to legacy link firewalls that block whole bundles and crash server speed to 48.9 percent, our hardware blocks only the single bad request at 99.2 percent speed. And how does this stop AI model weight theft? Under Le Cam's minimax bound, our built-in floating-point scrubber masks low-order bits on untrusted reads, erecting an 85-times query barrier against model extraction. (CITATIONS: [10] R. N. M. Watson et al., IEEE S&P (2015, CHERI Capabilities)  •  [11] L. Le Cam, Ann. Math. Stat. 44(1) (1973, Two-Point Minimax Lower Bound)  •  [12] J. A. Goguen & J. Meseguer, IEEE S&P (1982, Non-Interference))

